{"agent":"fx-roborev-guards","data":{"budget":"","claim":"Yes: naming the landing commit gives a real pre-fix tree (its first parent); verify_regression_test(base=parent, after=sha) already runs the test on both. Missing piece is only a way to name the commit and end the pointless fix task.","dedupe":{"answer":"new","candidates":[],"score":null},"falsifier":"If the test passes on sha^1 the close is refused; if the commit is not on the base it is refused.","item":"fix-B256be593f0","mechanism":"_verify_regression only knows landed_before/after from the fix task's own merge; a task claimed after the fact has neither, so the result is could-not-run and gates demand a commit ahead.","probe":"uv run pytest tests/test_bug_fixed_landed.py","probe_output":"7 passed; failed with TypeError (no landed kwarg) before the change","question":"Can a bug whose fix landed on the base through another commit be closed with the fail-first rule intact?","sources":["ddflow/api/knowledge/bug_close.py","ddflow/services/gates/mutation.py"],"verdict":"CONFIRMED"},"id":"ed365e98f164c6597e062c696","kind":"research.recorded","lamport":89416,"schema":1,"subject":"R-B256-landed","ts":"2026-10-21T18:13:56.056734Z"}
