{"agent":"fx-roborev-guards","data":{"by":"deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-V4-Pro-0813-Godzila","evidence":{"chunk_findings":[{"chunk":1,"detail":"When `--landed` is not supplied, the code sets `landing = (None, \"\")`. A non-empty tuple is truthy, so the later `**({\"landed\": landing[0]} if landing else {})` and `if landing: dropped = _end_fix_task_landed(log, item, landing[0])` both execute on ordinary bug closures. Concretely, `ddflow bug fixed <item> --regression-test <test>` now records `\"landed\": None` in the `bug.fixed` event and calls `_end_fix_task_landed(log, item, None)`, even though there is no landing commit. The condition should test the supplied commit (`landing[0]` / `landed.strip()`), not the tuple itself.","digest":"2686759e2b70c6e0","location":"ddflow/api/knowledge/bug_close.py: close_bug","severity":"HIGH","title":"ddflow/api/knowledge/bug_close.py: close_bug"},{"chunk":4,"detail":"The helper writes `tests/test_f.py` only at lines 29-31, immediately before committing the change returned as `landed` at line 32. The landed commit's first parent therefore contains no `tests/test_f.py`. Running `pytest -q tests/test_f.py::test_f` against that parent yields a file-not-found pytest usage error (exit code 4), so the verifier's prefix outcome should be `could-not-run`, not `\"failed\"`. Consequently `test_a_fix_landed_elsewhere_is_verified_against_its_landing_commit` (line 51) will fail on correctly fixed code, or the implementation must misclassify a missing test file as a legitimate test failure to make it pass. Either way the new regression test is invalid for the scenario it claims to test.","digest":"7511725dd42ffa8b","location":"tests/test_bug_fixed_landed.py:_landed_elsewhere","severity":"HIGH","title":"tests/test_bug_fixed_landed.py:_landed_elsewhere"}],"chunks_total":4,"coverage":"4/4 chunk(s) reviewed","delta_rounds":0,"diff_chars":17145,"diff_sha":"c01b6f82cafd769e415e709ed492ff9c70d7e41e782d9c2e19368ac7ca0c8ab2","diff_source":"main..HEAD + tracked wo